Backport of pipewire master 97699d707df374773fe5d6e32ea7362ef8dcf51b
("gstpipewiresrc: do not restart the stream when the running caps still fit")
to the 1.6.11 release tree. The commit is not on the 1.6 branch yet, so it is
hand-carried instead of taken with the rest of the series.

gst_pipewire_src_negotiate() compares the fixed current caps against the first
structure of the peer's caps, which can still hold ranges. Fixed caps never
compare equal to a range, so the check never fires and every reconfigure event
disconnects and reconnects the stream. Our caps carry the preferred value as a
list in front of a range, so they were always unfixated here.

The restart itself is the crash: it tears the buffer pool down while the gtk4
sink still holds a queued buffer, and the sink's drain copy then reads a memory
mapping that no longer exists. Measured on the tablet: the stream cycles
unconnected/connecting/paused/streaming about fifteen times in the first
seconds, then snapshot dies in gst_base_sink_drain.

--- a/src/gst/gstpipewiresrc.c
+++ b/src/gst/gstpipewiresrc.c
@@ -1144,7 +1144,11 @@
     current_caps = gst_pad_get_current_caps (GST_BASE_SRC_PAD (pwsrc));
     preferred_new_caps = gst_caps_copy_nth (possible_caps, 0);

-    if (current_caps && gst_caps_is_equal (current_caps, preferred_new_caps)) {
+    /* The caps we are already running are still acceptable downstream: a reconfigure
+     * that does not actually ask for anything different should not cost a stream
+     * restart. Comparing against the peer's first structure alone is not enough,
+     * because it can still hold ranges and never compares equal to fixed caps. */
+    if (current_caps && gst_caps_is_subset (current_caps, possible_caps)) {
       GST_DEBUG_OBJECT (pwsrc,
                         "Stream running and new caps equal current ones. "
                         "Skipping renegotiation.");
